</figure>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond demonstrating that the RL fine-tuning procedure enables the learning of effective motion primitives, we further analyze results obtained from comparing the pre-trained and fine-tuned models. As shown in Tab. <a href="#A5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ E.3.1 Learning Motion Primitives ‣ E.3 Supplementary Quantitative Results ‣ Appendix E Additional Simulation Experiment Details ‣ ComposableNav: Instruction-Following Navigation in Dynamic Environments via Composable Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the pre-trained model, which is trained to generate collision-free, goal-reaching trajectories, consistently achieves these objectives across all evaluated motion primitives. In particular, the pre-trained model achieves a 100% success rate for the <em id="A5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“Avoid walking through a region”</em> motion primitive. This outcome is anticipated, as avoiding designated regions is closely aligned with the collision avoidance objective emphasized during the pre-training phase.</p>
</div>
